      "value": "@{63f2ff3ae87fb64515e2e34e|full_name}: Yes it's *theoretically* possible to have data from one engine moved on another, but it's not *practical*. You are underestimating the complexity because you are ignorant of how both engines work, and of the complexities of 3D graphics in general.\n\n>> at it's core and 3D Models\n\nYou're being overly reductionist. Avatars and worlds are composed of higher-level abstractions that would either require complex conversion (e.g. animations) or essentially emulating Unity's render pipeline (e.g. shaders, which are *heavily* dependent on the external engine architecture to run).\n\nPhysbones are a VRChat component and have nothing to do with the engine. The paucity of features is a deliberate decision likely related to performance and network synchronisation considerations, as well as simplicity of setup. VRChat Players love their Physbones—sometimes a bit too much.\n\n>> Unreal Engine is way more efficient in everything than Unity\n\nBut what does that even mean in the context of VRChat? Are you saying that the 30-material 500k poly avatar-monstrosities that are regularly uploaded would suddenly become faster in Unreal? Perhaps you could enlighten us on how?\n\n>> bigger graphic advantage\n\nTake a look around VRChat at the graphically-stylised worlds and avatars and you will see that this is largely irrelevant.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
